    // — D338 enumerated fifteen readers off the `BUILT.attack` figures and this one
    // is keyed on the registry's INSERTION ORDER instead, so no grep of the census
    // numbers could have reached it. **THE COUNT THAT BITES IS THE ONE KEYED ON
    // SOMETHING YOU DID NOT THINK YOU WERE MOVING.** It is also, again, the only
    // rung in this `it` that could report: the two `length` assertions below sit
    // AFTER it, so a single run names this one alone.
    // 🆕🆕 D342 — the last raw key moves from `svp-117` to **`sv08.5-104`**, the
    // third Ciphermaniac's printing, appended after D340's Froslass trio. ⚠️ **THIS
    // RUNG HAS NOW GONE RED ON AN UNTOUCHED CONSTANT THREE TIMES (D338, D340, D342)
    // AND IT WAS ON THE PREDICTED LIST THIS TIME** — because D338's own paragraph
    // here names the mechanism (INSERTION ORDER, not a figure) rather than the
    // slice, which is the difference between a residue note that pays and one that
    // rots. It stood still at D341 for the honest reason: that slice authored a
    // READER and no registry row at all.
    // 🆕🆕 D345 — the last raw key moves from `sv08.5-104` to **`sv08.5-037`**, the
    // Prismatic Evolutions Dusknoir, appended after D342's Ciphermaniac's trio.
    // ⚠️ **THIS RUNG HAS NOW GONE RED ON AN UNTOUCHED CONSTANT FOUR TIMES (D338,
    // D340, D342, D345) AND IT WAS ON THE PREDICTED LIST AGAIN** — for D342's
    // reason verbatim: the paragraph above names the MECHANISM (insertion order,
    // not a figure), and a residue note that names a mechanism is spendable where
    // one that names a slice is not. 🛑 **AND THIS SLICE PAID THE SAME LESSON
    // TWICE**: `programPlayable`'s `damageChosen` arm (cardplay.ts) was likewise
    // keyed on something nobody thought they were moving — the opponent's BENCH
    // LENGTH standing in for an op's candidate set — and only the FIRST registry
